A||||2||deceler8||2.5 engine MAP sensor question...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||09-28-2000||06:29 PM||||While diagnosing a EGR problem, I disconnected the electrical connector from my MAP sensor and lo and behold, the engine's idle immediately smoothed out (very noticeably).<P>My question is...does this mean my MAP sensor is bad...and is this same MAP sensor used on other cars...the sensor has not caused any fault codes to date.<P>Gotta visit the wrecking yard for some EGR's...maybe I'll grab some MAP sensors too. Anyone know whether the sensor for an '86 2.5 engine is interchangeable with those on other cars ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-28-2000||09:11 PM||||It smoothed out partly because the ECM dropped into error mode. <P>the MAP on all the 2.5's is the same. check the vacuum line going to the MAP for blockage and leaks. that hard stuff can crack and get a real small leak which will screw up MAP. check everything else for vac leaks as well.<P>check the coolant temp sensor and wires. If that's damaged it will screwup idle etc. the old style sensor is real prone to corrosion in the connector.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||09-28-2000||10:33 PM||||I did all the vacuum leak things you have discussed (clamp PCV hose, clamp fittings, etc) and in addition torqued down my throttle body and intake manifold, new EGR gasket...cold idle is now 1200 vs 1500 before I did all this.<P>I'm sure the MAP is original on this car...seems crazy that idle would be better in an error condition. Maybe my error condition was plugging it back in (LOL).||||reg||1
